{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: forecasts are systematically too high vs realized, especially beyond ~day 10. Apply a downward level shift that grows with horizon (small in days 1–5, larger in days 10–30).",
    "Overestimated rebounds/mean reversion: after a downtrend, the model predicts recovery to low/mid-70s that does not materialize. Damp mean-reversion strength by scaling forecast deviations from the recent level (e.g., shrink toward the last price).",
    "Horizon-dependent overshoot: errors increase with lead time; long-horizon paths drift upward while realized stays lower. Add a horizon penalty (negative drift) or cap long-horizon upside relative to recent prices.",
    "Lag/slow recognition of regime shift: when the market transitions to a lower range (~60s), the model remains anchored in the prior higher regime. Use a regime filter: if last 5–10 days are below the 30-day average, shift the entire forecast path down and reduce slope.",
    "Excess volatility in the forecast path: model produces higher peaks and less drawdown than realized. Compress forecast amplitude (reduce day-to-day swings) and enforce stronger downside continuation when recent momentum is negative."
  ]
}
